\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity, yet the causal relationship between ambiguity type and safety violations remains unmeasured. We present a matched-pair study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 clause pairs spanning a six-type ambiguity taxonomy, evaluated across five models (2,997 episodes). Three findings emerge. First, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 percentage points (OR $= 15.1$), with a perturbation control supporting ambiguity-specific causation. Second, per-type effects range from $+$18.7\,pp (coreferential) to $+$49.2\,pp (incompleteness, upper bound including information-addition effects); five confound-controlled types span $+$18.7 to $+$36.0\,pp, forming a risk gradient rather than sharp tiers, with rankings partly margin-dependent. Third, error analysis of 614 violation traces reveals \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} ($\leq$11\% per-model awareness, 4.6\% overall) and \emph{systematic nocuity}---41.3\% of clauses triggering model-convergent violations---indicating clause-level rather than model-level risk factors. These results---observed in customer-service policy domains---motivate type-aware policy auditing and externalized ambiguity detection for policy-governed deployments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A customer asks to return a customized item within the 30-day window. The agent consults the store policy, which states that ``items may be returned within 30 days.'' The word ``items'' scopes over all product categories, including custom orders that are normally final sale. The agent grants a full refund and arranges free return shipping. One word of scopal ambiguity costs the retailer \$200.

LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that govern their permitted actions. These specifications inevitably contain ambiguity: a property that has been studied for decades in requirements engineering (RE) and computational linguistics. \citet{berry_kamsties_2004} distinguish between \emph{nocuous} (i.e., harmful) ambiguity, where different stakeholders arrive at different interpretations, and \emph{innocuous} ambiguity, where shared organizational context ensures convergent interpretation. In human RE workflows, most ambiguity is innocuous because developers share domain knowledge, can consult colleagues, and iteratively clarify requirements.

LLM agents lack these disambiguation channels. They cannot consult colleagues, rarely escalate to users, and must resolve ambiguity in real time from the text alone. This raises a question that has not been empirically addressed: does policy ambiguity cause safety violations in LLM agents, and if so, does the \emph{type} of ambiguity predict the severity of the violation? Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies \citep{drip_r_2026,li_ambiguity_taxonomy_2024} or measures policy understanding quality \citep{policybank_2026}, but no existing study provides a causal, type-granularity mapping from ambiguity to safety violations.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{Experimental overview. We construct 300 matched clause pairs across a six-type, two-layer ambiguity taxonomy and inject them into $\tau^2$-bench policy documents. Five models interact with the modified policies across 2,997 episodes. A cross-judge evaluation pipeline classifies violation severity on a four-tier scale.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We address this gap with a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench \citep{tau2_bench_2025}, a dual-control conversational agent benchmark. Using a six-type taxonomy organized into two layers---linguistic and specification, including specification-level gaps that produce equivalent behavioral effects in LLM agents (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), we construct 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs and evaluate five architecturally diverse models across 2,997 episodes.

Our results show that all policy ambiguity is nocuous for LLM agents ($+34.0$ pp violation rate increase, OR $= 15.1$), with type primarily determining severity. Incompleteness is consistently high (46--62\% across all models); an exploratory model$\times$type interaction (\S\ref{sec:c4}) suggests differential vulnerability across type--model combinations. Agents exhibit disambiguation neglect in over 89\% of cases, with fewer than 11\% showing any disambiguation awareness (4.6\% overall; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).

Our contributions are: (1)~the first matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity's safety impact with perturbation control (\S\ref{sec:method}), observed across five primary models and confirmed in two additional replication models; (2)~incompleteness as a consistent upper-bound risk type across all models and margins, with a variance decomposition showing that individual clause wording (22.4\%) dominates type membership (0.4\%) and model identity (1.0\%)---motivating clause-level auditing while the type taxonomy retains value for differential intervention design (\S\ref{sec:discussion}); and (3)~an error analysis quantifying \emph{Disambiguation Neglect} in consequential agentic settings---extending LLM ambiguity-collapse findings \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} to agentic settings---with $\leq$11\% per-model awareness (4.6\% overall), and \emph{systematic nocuity}: 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations, indicating clause-level risk factors (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

